<html>
<title>Direct Assembler</title>
<body>
<h1>Direct Assembler</h1>

<p>
The Direct Assembler allows assembling and disassembling code directly on the emulated computer.
</p>

<p>
The main elements of the Direct Assembler tab are shown in the screenshot below.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Direct Assembler</strong> tab</li>
</ul>

<p>
To open this tab, select the <strong>Direct Assembler</strong> tab (1).
</p>

<p>
When an emulator is running, the left half of the Direct Assembler tab displays a section of memory.
</p>

<p>
The different memory types are shown in the screenshot below.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_types_light.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1) - Undefined memory, i.e., no ROM or RAM present (shown in <font color="#c62828">red</font>)</li>
<li>(2) - RCA CDP1802, CDP1804, or CDP1805 code (shown in black/<font color="#EA6F32">orange</font>)</li>
<li>(3) - Branches (shown in <font color="#EA6F32">orange</font>)</li>
<li>(4) - Subroutine calls or big-endian (high byte first) 16-bit values (shown in <font color="#b251a7">pink</font>)</li>
<li>(5) - Little-endian (low byte first) 16-bit values (shown in <font color="#BB443F">red</font>)</li>
<li>(6) - Text (shown in <font color="#A85BA5">purple</font>)</li>
<li>(7) - Data (shown in <font color="#3947CE">blue</font>)</li>
<li>(8) - Pseudo-code such as Chip-8, ST2, ST4, and FEL (shown in <font color="#5C9597">steel blue</font>)</li>
<li>(9) - Macros (shown in black/<font color="#EA6F32">orange</font> or black/<font color="#b251a7">pink</font>)</li>
</ul>


<p>
In dark mode, the colours are the same except that Data is shown in <font color="#dccc8b">yellow</font>.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_types_dark.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<p>
The colours <font color="#EA6F32">orange</font> and <font color="#b251a7">pink</font> are also used for addresses that are <font color="#fc5900">branch</font> targets or <font color="#b251a7">subroutine call</font> targets. This makes it easier to see where the code will branch or jump to. For example, address 1403 is shown in <font color="#EA6F32">orange</font> because it is a branch target, and address 1400 is shown in <font color="#b251a7">pink</font> because it is a subroutine call target.
</p>

<p>
All memory types listed above may appear within a single session. However, pseudo-code is only available on machines running a dedicated pseudo interpreter. Emma 02 displays the correct type when:
</p>

<ol>
<li>The code was entered using the Direct <a href="DirectAssembler.htm">Assembler</a>.</li>
<li>The specified location was marked as a specific type (see <a href="DirectMarking.htm">Memory Type Selection</a>).</li>
<li>The specified location was executed in the emulator as RCA 180x or pseudo-code.</li>
</ol>

<p>
If no type is specified and no code has been executed, memory is displayed as <strong>Data</strong> or <strong>Undefined</strong>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> None of the settings in the Direct Assembler tab are saved in the Emma 02 configuration. Each time the emulator starts, these settings are reset to their default values.
</p>

